                'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A beginner web-development portfolio is a small collection of work that shows what you have practised and how you think through a problem. It is not a promise of employment. It is a learning record that can help you review progress, explain decisions and identify the next skill you need to build.</p>
<p>Golden Eye Academy’s active <a href="/courses/professional-web-development">web-development course page</a> lists the technologies and learning areas currently associated with the course. Your portfolio should grow from the work you genuinely understand, not from copied projects you cannot explain.</p>

<h2>Start with small, complete work</h2>
<p>A simple finished website can demonstrate more than a large unfinished idea. Begin with a clear purpose: a personal profile, a course information page, a small business layout or a form-based interface. Focus on readable content, consistent navigation and a layout that works on different screen sizes.</p>
<p>Completing the full path—from planning to testing—teaches you where problems appear. It also gives you a project you can improve as your HTML, CSS and programming skills develop.</p>

<h2>Show what each project demonstrates</h2>
<p>For every project, write a short explanation in your own words:</p>
<ul>
<li>What problem or user need did the project address?</li>
<li>Which technologies did you use?</li>
<li>Which parts did you build yourself?</li>
<li>What was difficult, and how did you investigate it?</li>
<li>How did you test the page or feature?</li>
<li>What would you improve next?</li>
</ul>
<p>This explanation matters because web work involves reasoning as well as visible design. A visitor looking at a screenshot cannot automatically see how you organized the code or solved a technical issue.</p>

<h2>Build quality before adding quantity</h2>
<p>You do not need dozens of projects. A smaller collection of understandable work is easier to maintain. Check spelling, broken links, mobile layout, form labels and basic accessibility. Remove placeholder content and make sure the project has a clear beginning, middle and end for the user.</p>
<p>As you learn server-side development and databases, you may add projects that store and display information. Keep the scope appropriate to your current level. A working basic feature is more useful for learning than a complex feature held together by code you do not understand.</p>

<h2>Protect private and sensitive information</h2>
<p>Never publish passwords, secret keys, private documents or real personal data in a portfolio. Use sample information when demonstrating forms and databases. Review configuration files before sharing code, and understand which files should remain private.</p>
<p>If a project is not safe to place online, use selected screenshots and a clear explanation instead. Safety is part of professional technical practice.</p>

<h2>Keep a record of your learning process</h2>
<p>Save notes about problems you solved, concepts you want to revisit and feedback you received. Version control can help you see how a project changed over time. Even without a public code profile, an organized local record helps you return to earlier work and continue improving it.</p>

<h2>Compare the course with your goal</h2>
<p>Before enrolling, read the published course outline and consider whether the listed technologies match what you want to learn. Ask about the starting level, current batch timing and how the listed learning areas are practised. Do not assume that a course title alone defines the exact portfolio you will finish.</p>
<h2>Review the portfolio as a visitor would</h2>
<p>Ask another person to open a project without explaining it first. Can they understand the purpose, move between pages, read the text on a phone and tell when an action succeeds or fails? Their confusion can reveal gaps you no longer notice because you know the project too well.</p>
<p>Then review the work yourself with a simple checklist: page title, navigation, headings, labels, keyboard access, mobile layout, empty states and error messages. Check that external links open as intended and that forms never appear to submit when nothing happened. These details may look small, but together they show whether you can finish and test a complete user journey.</p>
<p>Keep one short project note with the date, current version and next improvement. This prevents a portfolio from becoming a set of forgotten files and helps you decide whether the next learning step is design, frontend behaviour, server-side logic, databases or deployment practice.</p>
<p>Explore the broader <a href="/job-computer-skills">computer and IT learning path</a>, or <a href="/join-now?course=professional-web-development&amp;selected_course=professional-web-development&amp;source_page=blog&amp;source_section=web-portfolio-guide&amp;inquiry_intent=course_guidance">Ask for Course Help</a> about the current web-development class.</p>
