                'image' => 'site/img/jlpt-n5.jpg',
                'author' => 'Golden Eye Academy',
                'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>JLPT N5 preparation begins with a foundation in Japanese scripts, vocabulary, grammar, reading and listening. The goal of an early study plan is not to rush through every topic. It is to build a routine that helps you recognize and use basic language more reliably over time.</p>
<p>Golden Eye Academy lists its active Japanese classes in the <a href="/courses-all?category=language-classes">language course category</a>. Use official JLPT information for current exam rules and use the course pages to compare learning options.</p>

<h2>Learn Hiragana and Katakana carefully</h2>
<p>Hiragana and Katakana are essential reading tools. Learn a manageable group of characters, practise writing them and read short combinations aloud. Avoid relying for too long on Romanized spelling because it can slow down direct recognition of Japanese scripts.</p>
<p>Review characters in mixed order, not only in the sequence you first memorized. This helps you recognize them in real words rather than only in a chart.</p>

<h2>Build vocabulary in simple context</h2>
<p>A word is easier to remember when it is connected to a sentence, image or everyday situation. Group vocabulary around familiar topics such as time, family, food, places, study and daily actions. Revisit old vocabulary while adding new words.</p>
<ul>
<li>Read and write the word.</li>
<li>Listen to a clear example.</li>
<li>Use it in a short sentence.</li>
<li>Record mistakes in a review list.</li>
<li>Return to the word several days later.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Study grammar as a pattern with meaning</h2>
<p>Grammar should help you understand how a sentence works. Learn what a pattern means, notice its word order and practise it with vocabulary you already know. Compare similar patterns and write your own short examples instead of memorizing a translation alone.</p>
<p>Ask for feedback when a sentence feels uncertain. Correcting a small misunderstanding early can prevent it from becoming a repeated habit.</p>

<h2>Develop reading and listening gradually</h2>
<p>Begin with short material that uses familiar vocabulary and grammar. When reading, identify the topic before focusing on every detail. When listening, first notice the situation and key words, then listen again for specific information.</p>
<p>Do not expect to understand every word immediately. Use each practice activity to identify the gap you need to review next.</p>

<h2>Use official JLPT information</h2>
<p>Exam dates, registration, fees, venues, rules and score information can change. Check the responsible official JLPT sources for current details. Preparation articles and classes should support your learning, not replace official exam information.</p>

<h2>Make a realistic weekly routine</h2>
<p>Divide your study time across scripts, vocabulary, grammar, reading and listening. A simple routine may include a small daily review and one longer weekly session to revisit difficult areas. The exact schedule should fit your available time and current pace.</p>
<p>Keep a record of what you studied. At the end of each week, identify one area that improved and one area that needs more attention.</p>

<h2>Compare the available Japanese courses</h2>
<p>The <a href="/courses/jlpt-n5-elite">Japanese Proficiency JLPT N5</a> page describes a foundation starting point. Learners with stronger basics can also review the <a href="/courses/jlpt-n4">JLPT N4 course</a>. Read the descriptions and outlines before deciding which level to discuss.</p>
<h2>Use simple checks to guide the next week</h2>
<p>At the end of a study week, choose a few characters, words and grammar patterns without looking at your notes. Read them, write a short example and listen for them in a simple recording. The result is not a formal score; it is a practical signal about what you remember and what needs another round of review.</p>
<p>Keep the check small enough to repeat. If recognition is improving but writing remains difficult, adjust the next week rather than abandoning the plan. If a grammar pattern is still unclear, collect two or three simple examples and ask about the difference. A routine that responds to your actual mistakes is more useful than moving forward only because a calendar says you should.</p>
<p>Use the same idea for listening. Replay a short section, write the words or details you recognized and compare them with a reliable transcript or explanation when available. Notice whether the difficulty came from vocabulary, speed, pronunciation or attention. That distinction makes the next practice session much more focused.</p>
<p>Small, specific observations make it easier to ask a useful class question.</p>
<p>Share your current script knowledge, previous study and preferred timing with the academy. Then <a href="/join-now?course=jlpt-n5-elite&amp;selected_course=jlpt-n5-elite&amp;source_page=blog&amp;source_section=jlpt-n5-guide&amp;inquiry_intent=course_guidance">Ask for Course Help</a> about the current Japanese class.</p>
