                'image' => 'site/img/about.jpg',
                'author' => 'Golden Eye Academy',
                'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Parents often help students compare fees, schedules, safety, faculty and future value. A useful academy decision should also begin with the student’s present need. The right questions make it easier to move beyond advertising and understand what the student will actually study.</p>
<p>Golden Eye Academy has provided courses, classes and academic support in Pokhara since 2008. Parents can start with the <a href="/for-parents">parent course-information page</a> and use this checklist when discussing a class with the academy.</p>

<h2>Start with the student’s goal</h2>
<p>Ask the student what they want to improve and why. The answer may involve language confidence, exam preparation, computer skills, office work, web development or another academic need. A clear goal helps the family compare relevant courses instead of selecting a course only because it is popular.</p>
<p>Also discuss the student’s interest. Parent support is valuable, but the learner must attend, practise and continue when the work becomes difficult.</p>

<h2>Read the published course information together</h2>
<p>Open the relevant course page and review the description, learning areas, duration, current fee and listed faculty information. Ask the student to explain what they think the course covers. This quickly reveals whether both of you understand the option in the same way.</p>
<p>If the course description does not answer an important question, write it down for the academy team. A short prepared list makes an inquiry or visit more productive.</p>

<h2>Ask about the current learning experience</h2>
<ul>
<li>Which current batch is suitable for the student’s level?</li>
<li>Who is expected to teach that batch?</li>
<li>What kind of practice supports the published learning areas?</li>
<li>What attendance and home practice are expected?</li>
<li>How is progress discussed during the course?</li>
<li>What certificate information applies to this specific course?</li>
</ul>
<p>These questions focus the discussion on the student experience rather than a broad claim.</p>

<h2>Check the practical commitment</h2>
<p>Compare the batch timing with school, college, travel and family responsibilities. A schedule should leave the student enough energy for practice and regular attendance. Discuss the full listed duration and current fee so the family can plan the commitment from the beginning.</p>
<p>If the student needs equipment, books or software, understand that before classes start. Prepared learners can focus more quickly on the actual learning work.</p>

<h2>Keep expectations realistic</h2>
<p>A course can provide teaching, structure, guided practice and academic support. It cannot replace the learner’s effort or guarantee a score, admission, visa, job or placement. Ask what the student should be able to understand or practise through the published course areas, and discuss how progress will depend on continued participation.</p>

<h2>Review trust evidence carefully</h2>
<p>Check that the academy name, Pokhara location and contact information are consistent. Review active faculty and course pages. Treat testimonials as individual experiences, not promises that every learner will have the same result. If a claim materially affects your decision, ask for clear information rather than relying on an impression.</p>

<h2>Include the student in the final decision</h2>
<p>After gathering the details, ask the student to explain why the course fits their goal and how they will manage the timetable and practice. A shared decision creates clearer expectations for both the learner and the family.</p>
<h2>Agree on support after classes begin</h2>
<p>A parent does not need to monitor every exercise, but the family can agree on a simple way to discuss the student’s routine. Ask what the learner practised, what felt difficult and what help they need to continue. Focus on attendance, effort and understanding instead of comparing the student with another learner.</p>
<p>Set a reasonable point to review the decision together. The conversation can consider whether the timetable remains workable, whether the student understands the learning focus and whether any question should be raised with the academy. This keeps parent support practical without turning every class into a promise about a future result.</p>
<p>Keep fee receipts, course information and important messages organized. Clear records help the family remember what was discussed and reduce confusion later. If circumstances change, return to the academy with the specific question and the relevant course details.</p>
<p>This creates a calm, shared basis for future course conversations.</p>
<p>Browse the <a href="/courses-all">active Golden Eye Academy courses</a> and review the <a href="/faq">academy FAQs</a>. When you want to discuss a specific student goal, <a href="/join-now?course=undecided&amp;selected_course=undecided&amp;source_page=blog&amp;source_section=parent-checklist&amp;audience_type=parent&amp;inquiry_intent=parent_course_guidance">Ask for Course Help</a>.</p>
HTML,
                'meta_keywords' => 'choose an academy Pokhara, parent course checklist, Golden Eye Academy, courses for students',
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Questions to Ask Before Enrolling in a Course',
                'slug' => 'why-you-should-ask-before-enrollment',
                'category' => 'Course Guidance',
                'image' => 'site/img/cat-4.jpg',
                'author' => 'Golden Eye Academy',
                'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A course inquiry is more useful when it goes beyond “When does the class start?” The course focus, starting level, fee, duration, faculty and learning expectations all shape whether a class fits your goal. Asking a few prepared questions can make the enrollment decision clearer for both students and parents.</p>
<p>Golden Eye Academy provides <a href="/courses-all">active course pages</a> and Course Help so learners in Pokhara can understand the available options before choosing a batch.</p>

<h2>What do I want to learn?</h2>
<p>Begin by describing the skill, subject or exam preparation you need. Avoid using only a broad statement such as “I want a good course.” Explain what you find difficult now and what you hope to do more confidently after consistent learning and practice.</p>
<p>Your answer helps separate courses that may have similar audiences but different learning areas.</p>

<h2>Does this course match my starting level?</h2>
<p>Read the description and outline, then explain what you already know. A learner new to computer work may need a different starting point from someone who already uses office applications. A language beginner may need foundation work before an exam-focused class.</p>
<p>Ask whether the current batch begins at a level you can follow and what practice is expected between classes.</p>

<h2>What exactly does the course cover?</h2>
<p>Use the published outline as the starting point. Identify the learning areas that matter most to you and ask how they connect across the course. Do not assume that every possible topic in a broad subject is included.</p>
<ul>
<li>Which learning areas receive the most class time?</li>
<li>What kind of guided practice supports those areas?</li>
<li>What should students review independently?</li>
<li>How does the learning progress from the beginning to later topics?</li>
</ul>

<h2>What are the current fee, duration and batch options?</h2>
<p>Course pages show the listed fee and duration. Ask the academy team for the current batch timing and seat information. Consider the total time commitment, travel and practice routine alongside the fee.</p>
<p>A batch that fits your weekly schedule can support more consistent attendance. If you have school, college or work responsibilities, share the times that are genuinely realistic for you.</p>

<h2>Who is associated with the current class?</h2>
<p>Review the listed faculty information and ask who is expected to teach the batch you are considering. The teaching arrangement may be especially important when a course covers several learning areas.</p>

<h2>How will I practise and understand my progress?</h2>
<p>Different subjects use different activities. A language class may include listening, speaking, reading or writing practice. A computer class may use guided tasks and completed files. Ask how the current course approaches practice and feedback without assuming that one method applies everywhere.</p>

<h2>Does a certificate apply to this course?</h2>
<p>Certificate availability and completion requirements can differ by course. If a certificate matters to your decision, ask for the specific details before enrolling rather than relying on a general statement.</p>

<h2>What should I expect from the course?</h2>
<p>A course can provide instruction, structured learning, practice and academic support. Individual progress also depends on attendance, starting level and continued effort. Be cautious of promises about scores, visas, admissions, jobs or placements.</p>

<h2>Prepare a concise inquiry</h2>
<p>Share your current level, main goal, preferred timing and the course you are considering. If a parent is involved in the decision, include the questions that matter to the family. You do not need to send unrelated personal information.</p>
<h2>Compare the answers before deciding</h2>
<p>Write the important answers beside the course information you reviewed online. Check that the fee, duration, learning areas and current batch make sense together. If an answer is unclear, ask one direct follow-up question instead of assuming what it means. Parents and students can use the same notes so the final decision is based on a shared understanding.</p>
<p>Give yourself enough time to consider the weekly commitment. A course that fits the goal but conflicts with school, college, work or travel may be difficult to follow consistently. The best next step is one you understand, can reasonably attend and are prepared to practise beyond the classroom.</p>
<p>Once the details are clear, choose deliberately and keep a copy of the course information you used for the decision.</p>
<p>Review the <a href="/faq">Golden Eye Academy FAQs</a> for common questions. When you are ready to discuss your own goal, <a href="/join-now?course=undecided&amp;selected_course=undecided&amp;source_page=blog&amp;source_section=before-enrollment-guide&amp;inquiry_intent=course_guidance">Ask for Course Help</a>.</p>
HTML,
                'meta_keywords' => 'questions before course enrollment, course guidance Pokhara, Golden Eye Academy courses, choose a batch',
            ],
        ];
    }

